1. Restricted Sections for Crawling:
- Special pages (Special:Search, Special:Random, etc.)
- Administration pages (Wikipedia:Articles_for_deletion, Wikipedia:Requests_for_arbitration)
- User pages and user talk pages
- Discussion pages (all *_talk: namespaces)
- Copyright investigation pages
- Deletion-related pages (votes for deletion, deletion discussions)
- Administrator noticeboards and election pages
- Sandbox pages (Module:Sandbox, Template:Sandbox)
- Spam-related pages

2. Specific User Agent Rules:
- MJ12bot - completely disallowed
- Mediapartners-Google* (advertising bots) - disallowed
- IsraBot and Orthogaffe (Wikipedia work bots) - allowed
- UbiCrawler, DOC, Zao - disallowed
- Known problematic bots (HTTrack, WebCopier, Teleport, etc.) - all disallowed
- wget in recursive mode - disallowed unless properly configured

3. Purpose of robots.txt:
The robots.txt file serves as a communication protocol between websites and web crawlers, specifying which areas should not be accessed. 
It promotes ethical scraping by:
- Protecting sensitive or private content from being indexed
- Preventing server overload from aggressive crawlers
- Maintaining website owners' control over their content distribution
- Providing clear guidelines for respectful data collection practices


This analysis shows that Wikipedia uses robots.txt to:

Protect sensitive discussion and administrative pages
Block known problematic crawlers
Allow well-behaved bots to access public content while respecting server resources